task: named-tuples-stage0
agent: fable51-core
started: 2026-09-12
branch: feature/named-tuples-stage0
worktree: ../okay-wt-namedtuples
spec: specs/ — a new section for the measurement (written and
  committed before the code, spec-dev); docs/typepedia.md gains the
  verdict whatever it is

why: operator, 2026-09-11/12 — after `into` and generalized method
  syntax, the third Scala 3 feature we have not evaluated. The target
  is named in our own code: `Gtfs.departures` carries a COMMENT on
  every line of its join chain saying what the tuple is
  (`// trip -> (time, (route, service))`), because the type does not.
  And `Csv.Row = Map[String, String]`, read by string key in 410
  places, where a typo is a runtime miss.

what — a MEASUREMENT, not a migration. Three questions, and the lane
  answers them on the real Wrocław job (the GTFS snapshot is on this
  box, 48 MB):
  1. does the number move? It must not: a named tuple is erased. Shown
     already in a probe (`(trip = "7", service = "w")` IS a
     `scala.Tuple2` at runtime and `==` to `("7", "w")`), so the lane
     proves it on OUR code by comparing the compiled twin, and by the
     timed stage if the box is quiet enough to mean anything.
  2. DOES INFERENCE SURVIVE our own API? This is the real risk and the
     reason the lane exists. `select[B](f: A => B)` and
     `join[B](r: Table[(K, B)]): Table[(K, (A, B))]` are generic; if
     a named payload needs an annotation at every step, the
     readability win is spent and the answer is no.
  3. how much of the 410 string-key reads would a DECLARED row type
     cover, without a macro that reads data files at compile time
     (which I would refuse anyway)?

how: `departuresNamed`, a twin of `Gtfs.departures` beside it, with
  the payloads named and the comments deleted BECAUSE the types say
  it. A test asserts the two produce identical output on the real
  feed — the strongest correctness check available here, and it makes
  the twin a fixture rather than a sketch.

predict: (1) yes, identical. (2) the coin toss, and the thing worth
  knowing: `join`'s `(K, A)` pairing is positional by signature, so
  names can only ride the PAYLOAD, and I expect the key half to stay
  ugly. (3) partial — the join keys and the columns actually read are
  a small, declarable set per file; the `week.map(r(_) == "1")` shape
  in calendar.txt reads a column by a computed name and cannot be
  declared at all. Recorded so being wrong shows.

verdict rule, set before the measurement: this lands as a RECORD
  either way. If (2) comes back needing annotations, the entry says
  so and nothing migrates.
